{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And the magicians said to Pharaoh, this is the finger of Elohim</b> Rabbi Abraham said: Because they did as Aaron's deed with the serpent (תנין) also in the plague of blood and the frogs, and now they were unable to do so, they said to Pharaoh: This plague did not come through Aaron on behalf of Israel, rather it is a plague of Elohim, according to the arrangement of the stars over the land of Egypt — for Pharaoh did not deny the Creator, only the Name that Moses mentioned to him. This is like the manner of 'for His hand has not struck us; it was chance that happened to us' (I Samuel 6:9); therefore Pharaoh's heart was hardened. And he brought proof from the fact that it does not say 'the finger of YHWH' — which is the God of Israel — as Pharaoh said in the plague of the frogs: 'Entreat YHWH.' Furthermore, Moses told Pharaoh about the plague of the river but did not mention the plague of the lice to him beforehand.\n\nBut his words are not correct in my eyes, for a chance occurrence would not be called <b>the finger of Elohim</b>; rather, a blow coming from Him as punishment is called 'the hand of YHWH' and 'the finger of Elohim,' as Scripture says that he brought: 'His hand has not struck us' (I Samuel 6:9), and it is written, 'And Israel saw the great hand' (Exodus 14:31), 'And the hand of YHWH will be upon you and your fathers' (I Samuel 12:15), 'The hand of Elohim was very heavy there' (ibid. 5:11). Furthermore, the magicians did not stand before Moses after that — also in the plague of the mixed swarm (ערוב), which came with a warning, and not in the remaining plagues.\n\nBut the matter is according to its plain sense: when the magicians saw that they could not bring forth the lice, they acknowledged that Aaron's deed was from Elohim; therefore Pharaoh did not call them from this time onward. And to diminish the matter they said <b>the finger of Elohim</b> and did not say 'the hand of Elohim' — meaning, a small blow from Him. And they did not say 'the finger of YHWH,' because Pharaoh and his servants would not mention the particular Name except when speaking with Moses, because he mentions it to them. And as for why the magicians could not bring forth the lice, there was a cause from HaShem: He confounded their counsel by His will, for all is His and all is in His hand.\n\nAnd what seems right in my eyes further is that the plague of blood — to transform the nature of the water into blood — and the plague of the frogs — to bring them up from the river — they were able to do, for there is in them no creation or formation. For Scripture does not say 'and the frogs became,' but rather 'and the frog came up' — they gathered and came up. But the plague of lice was an act of creation, and it is not the nature of dust to become lice; therefore it says 'and it became lice,' and it says 'to bring forth the lice,' like the matter of 'let the earth bring forth living souls, etc., and it was so' (Genesis 1:24). And no one can perform such a deed except the Creator, blessed and exalted be He. And it says, 'And they did so, but they could not' — for they adjured the demons to do their bidding, but they had no power.\n\nAnd what supports my words regarding the frogs is their saying (Shemot Rabbah 10:4): Rabbi Akiva says, there was one frog, and it spawned and filled all the land of Egypt. Rabbi Elazar ben Azarya said to him: What do you have to do with Aggadah? Go from your words and go to the laws of skin diseases and tents. There was one frog, and it croaked to them, and they came. For Rabbi Elazar ben Azarya did not see that the magicians could bring a new nature into the frog to reproduce multitudes beyond their nature, but rather they gathered them to bring them up. And according to the opinion of Rabbi Akiva, the river swarmed with frogs — that it swarmed more than its usual manner. And according to the opinion of Rabbi Elazar ben Azarya, it is like 'swarm upon the earth' (Genesis 9:7) — the matter of movement, as I explained in the portion of Bereshit (1:20): that they gathered and moved about in the river, and from there came up upon the Egyptians.\n\nAnd our Rabbis (Shemot Rabbah 10:7) said regarding the lice that a demon has no dominion over a creature smaller than a lentil, and they could not even gather lice from their place and bring them. And they said in Midrash Rabbah (ibid.) further: Once the magicians saw that they could not bring forth the lice, immediately they recognized that it was the deed of Elohim and not the deed of demons, and they no longer presumed to liken themselves to Moses to bring the plagues.\n\nAnd what Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 8:15), that Moses did not inform them of the plague of lice — it seems to me that Aaron struck with the staff before Pharaoh's eyes, as he did with the soot (Exodus 9:10), but he did not warn him. For the Holy One, blessed be He, does not warn Pharaoh except in plagues that involve death to a person, as it is written regarding the frogs: 'and the frog destroyed them' (Psalms 78:45) — which alludes to death or destruction that our Rabbis mentioned (Shemot Rabbah 10:4), that they would castrate them. Likewise the locusts would kill them through famine, for they ate the remainder of the provision left to them from the hail. And all is mercy from Him toward man, like the matter that is said (Ezekiel 33:9): 'And you, because you warned the wicked to turn from his way and he did not turn — he is the wicked, in his iniquity he shall die, but you have saved your soul.' Therefore He did not warn him regarding the lice, nor regarding the boils, nor regarding the darkness — only regarding the pestilence, because it is death, and it is fitting that it should come upon man as well, as He said to him afterward (Exodus 9:16), 'But for this purpose I have raised you up'; therefore He informed him how it would be."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "Where Ramban refers to 'Rabbi Abraham' and then clarifies with the parenthetical '(אבן עזרא על שמות ח׳:ט״ו)', this is Ibn Ezra. The citation of Psalms 78:45 appears as (תהלים עח מה) without quotation marks in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:05:14.491809810Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}